//! Linear ABI autopeli logic (traffic AI + player controls).
//! Host runs GamePhysics; this module reads/writes the shared RGW1 block and
//! authors an RGU1 HUD.
//!
//! The ABI plumbing (block layouts, exports, input handling, impulse/event
//! cursors) lives in the shared `ranger_game` crate
//! (gallery/game_engine/lib/ranger_game) — this file is only the game: road /
//! oil / ramp / traffic data, driving + feedback rules, and the HUD content.
//! The exports the host calls (abi_base / init / update / declare_resources,
//! plus rg_ui_ptr / rg_ui_size / rg_ui_revision) are generated by
//! `ranger_game::world_game!` and `ranger_game::ui_exports!` at the bottom.
//!
//! GAME vs GAME ENGINE — reading guide
//! -----------------------------------
//! Two kinds of code live in this file, and the comments call them out:
//!
//!   * GAME (this car game): all the data and rules that are only true for
//!     autopeli — the track/oil/ramp/traffic tables, the driving + feedback
//!     physics tuning, the body id-code taxonomy, the HUD content, and the
//!     meaning we assign to each control/scalar/event channel.
//!
//!   * GAME ENGINE INTERFACE (the environment): every place we cross into the
//!     shared `ranger_game` crate — the `World`/`Frame`/`Input` handles, the
//!     `resources`/`ui` builders, the `WorldGame` trait we implement, and the
//!     `world_game!`/`ui_exports!` macros. These are the engine's generic
//!     transport; they carry our bytes but assign them no meaning.
//!
//! Convention used below: a line tagged `// [engine]` is a call across the
//! Game Engine interface (the `ranger_game` crate / the shared RGW1·RGU1
//! blocks). Everything NOT so tagged is this game's own logic. Section banners
//! (`// == GAME ... ==`) mark whole blocks that are purely the game.

mod hud;

// [engine] The only things this game imports are the Game Engine's generic
// transport types/helpers; nothing here knows the word "autopeli".
use ranger_game::input::{Buttons, Input};
use ranger_game::world::{event, World, WorldGame};
use ranger_game::{resources, FP};

